## Food Recalls by the Numbers

As of this year, the total reported recalls have reached an unsettling 327 cases, with 90 classified as Class I, meaning a reasonable probability that the use of or exposure to the product will cause serious adverse health consequences or death. Compared to the 685 total recalls ever recorded, this year alone makes up nearly 48% of all historical recalls--a statistic that should give pause.

## Allergen Mislabeling Leads the Charge

Delving deeper into the data, allergen mislabeling represents the lion's share of this year's recalls with a staggering 184 cases, overshadowing salmonella contamination at 106, listeria at 113, and foreign object discoveries at 80. The data reveals that undeclared allergens, particularly nuts, milk, and soy, are common culprits.

## Repeat Offenders: A Disturbing Trend

67 repeat offenders have been noted this year, with some brands making the list as particularly problematic: Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons Companies LLC, both with 11 recalls, carry a dismal "F" rating. Most concerning, however, is CHETAK NEW YORK LLC, which has topped the charts with 52 recalls--all classified as Class I.

## Recent Recalls: What to Look Out For

Among the most recent incidents include undeclared allergens in Kirkland Signature Madeleines by Costco, lead contamination in Summit Import Corporation's Spice Powder, and foreign objects in Mondelez's CHIPS AHOY! Baked Bites Brookie. These cases highlight the breadth of issues that can arise, from mislabeling to contamination.

## Closing Thoughts

The recurring nature of some recalls suggests systemic issues that demand attention from manufacturers and oversight bodies. For consumers, vigilance is key. Regularly checking recall notices and knowing which brands have higher failure rates can help minimize risks.
